nly","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"circle-and-two-lines","label":"Split into two lines and count their circle intersections","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circle-and-two-lines":["cnm_224","cnm_h177","cnm_h179"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_224","cnm_h177","cnm_h179"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:9b63152ee3fd30b710327355"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 2+√2","reason":"Take A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(1,1), and D=(0,1). The condition becomes x²+(y+1)²=2. This circle has center (0,−1), radius √2, and center-to-D distance 2, so its greatest distance from D is 2+√2, attained at (0,−1−√2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h177"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"circle-and-triangle-inequality","label":"Circle locus and farthest point","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circle-and-triangle-inequality":["cnm_h177","cnm_111"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h177","cnm_111"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-the-distance-algebraically","label":"Coordinate inequality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-the-distance-algebraically":["cnm_h177","cnm_h024","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h177","cnm_h024","cnm_096"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_054"],"id":"find-circle-and-farthest-point","label":"Find the locus of P","methodDirectNodeIds":{"find-circle-and-farthest-point":["cnm_h173","cnm_h177","cnm_h176","cnm_111"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h173","cnm_h177","cnm_h176","cnm_111","cnm_082","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_054"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:9b63152ee3fd30b710327355"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 12 + 12√2","reason":"Let AC = a and BC = b. The perpendicular bisectors of XY and WZ are those of AB and AC, so the common circle's center is the midpoint of AB. Equating its squared 